  "worked_solution": "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x.\\,\u2713$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,\u2713$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x.\\,\u2713$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,\u2713$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},-1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
